## 2. `option_bag_junk_drawer` — `describeCheckout` (L10–19, agent_risk 0.39)

**Evidence:** *parameter: `options` · 6 distinct property reads: currency, plan, region, retry, role, status*

Worth being precise: this bag *is* typed (`CheckoutOptions`), so this isn't the usual `options: any` case. The charge still lands because the interface is one flat drawer of six unrelated concerns — plan/role (identity + authority), region/currency (locale), status/retry (attempt state) — and the function reads all six while owning none of them.

**What an agent gets wrong today:** the return value is a `:`-joined string whose **field order is a serialisation contract**, and nothing in the type says so. Asked to "include the coupon code in the checkout descriptor," the obvious edit is to add `couponCode` to the interface and append it to the array. That silently re-keys every string this function has ever produced — cache keys, log lines, analytics dimensions — and if the field is optional, `String(undefined)` writes the literal `"undefined"` into the middle of the key. The flat bag makes the edit look like a one-line addition when it's a format change.

**Refactor — split the drawer into the records it actually is, and name the output:**

```ts
type CheckoutIdentity = { plan: string; role: string };
type CheckoutLocale = { region: string; currency: string };
type CheckoutAttempt = { status: string; retry: boolean };

/** Colon-joined, order-significant. Consumers parse by position — do not reorder or insert. */
export type CheckoutKey = string & { readonly __brand: "CheckoutKey" };

export function describeCheckout(
  identity: CheckoutIdentity,
  locale: CheckoutLocale,
  attempt: CheckoutAttempt,
): CheckoutKey {
  return [
    identity.plan,
    identity.role,
    locale.region,
    locale.currency,
    attempt.status,
    String(attempt.retry),
  ].join(":") as CheckoutKey;
}
```

If the call sites can't be touched, the cheap version is to destructure in the signature — `function describeCheckout({ plan, role, region, currency, status, retry }: CheckoutOptions)` — which at least makes the read set visible at the top of the function instead of scattered through the body. The branded `CheckoutKey` is the part that actually protects the format: it gives the next agent somewhere to hang the "order is load-bearing" warning where they'll read it.

---

## 3. `negative_flag_maze` — `canRunBilling` (L34–35, agent_risk 0.37)

**Evidence:** *negative flags: `disableBilling`, `skipRetry` · condition: `!disableBilling && !skipRetry`*

Co-located with the fourth finding, `boolean_naming_drift` (crime_00004, both names @L34) — same root cause, and one refactor clears both.

Three problems stack here. The double negation (`!disable`, `!skip`) means the reader has to invert twice to get to "billing may run." The two params are both `boolean`, so **`canRunBilling(disableBilling, skipRetry)` typechecks with the arguments swapped** — nothing catches it. And the `if/return true/return false` shape hides that this is just a conjunction.

**What an agent gets wrong today:** asked to "also block billing when the account is suspended," the pattern-matching move is to follow the local style:

```ts
if (!disableBilling && !skipRetry && !accountActive) return true;   // wrong
```

The surrounding code trains the agent that every flag arrives negated, so it negates a positive one and inverts the new condition. Separately, reading `canRunBilling(true, false)` at a call site tells you nothing — the name says "can run," the `true` means "disabled."

**Refactor — positive names, object parameter, expression body:**

```ts
export type BillingPolicy = {
  isBillingEnabled: boolean;
  isRetryAllowed: boolean;
};

export function canRunBilling(policy: BillingPolicy): boolean {
  return policy.isBillingEnabled && policy.isRetryAllowed;
}
```

Call sites become `canRunBilling({ isBillingEnabled: true, isRetryAllowed: false })` — unswappable, and the extension is now the boring correct thing (`&& policy.isAccountActive`). Callers holding the old flags invert once, explicitly, at the boundary: `{ isBillingEnabled: !disableBilling, isRetryAllowed: !skipRetry }`.

One thing the refactor surfaces that the detector doesn't claim: once the names read positively, **"retry is not allowed" gating whether billing runs at all** looks wrong. A retry policy shouldn't veto a first attempt. Worth a question to whoever owns this before the rename lands — the negative names were hiding a possible logic bug, not just an ergonomics one.

---

